    },
    {
      "id": "3202b955-c51b-4b52-9168-b90ab886198f",
      "noteId": "e90d4692-4430-44b0-8497-d0668d30acec",
      "position": 11,
      "type": "PARAGRAPH",
      "text": "**Hint**\nWater molecules at the surface constantly gain energy from heat and their surroundings. When a molecule’s kinetic energy is high enough, it can break free into the air as vapour, even if the bulk liquid has not reached the boiling point.",
      "checked": 0,
      "indent": 0,
      "refObjectId": null,
      "language": null,
      "createdAtEpochMs": 1793358889628,
      "updatedAtEpochMs": 1793358889628
    },
    {
      "id": "49fa2812-6b59-42a8-bc94-8cbe1f22b3b7",
      "noteId": "e90d4692-4430-44b0-8497-d0668d30acec",
      "position": 12,
      "type": "PARAGRAPH",
      "text": "**Why**\nThe source explains that evaporation “can occur below the boiling point because some surface molecules have enough energy to escape.” Heat adds energy to these molecules, raising the chance that some will overcome the liquid’s surface tension and leave.",
      "checked": 0,
      "indent": 0,
      "refObjectId": null,
      "language": null,
      "createdAtEpochMs": 1793358889628,
      "updatedAtEpochMs": 1793358889628
    },
    {
      "id": "cc32685c-25a8-4685-b450-b6f917cfe220",
      "noteId": "e90d4692-4430-44b0-8497-d0668d30acec",
      "position": 13,
      "type": "PARAGRAPH",
      "text": "**Next step**\nThink of a glass of water left on a cool table. Why does the water level drop slowly over hours? Your answer may cite [source].",
      "checked": 0,
      "indent": 0,
      "refObjectId": null,
      "language": null,
      "createdAtEpochMs": 1793358889628,
      "updatedAtEpochMs": 1793358889628
    },
